fact WorkItem(id: "WI-189", description: some(value: "Reify/reflect operators (staging brackets). Add a paired pair of operators that make the term ↔ value boundary first-class: `↑e` (or `'{e}` / `quote(e)`) reifies a value-level expression to its Term representation; `↓t` (or `${t}` / `splice(t)`) reflects a Term back to a value at an inferred (or annotated) sort. Bracket-typing rule: in `↓[T] t`, t must be a Term whose head functor matches T's declaration; the typer elaborates field access as if `↓t` is a value of sort T, so `(↓t).status` becomes a typed projection rather than a stringly-typed `term_field` call. Reflexively in the other direction: `↑v` produces a Term with the loader-normalized form of v's runtime representation. The brackets subsume `term_field` / `term_as_string` / `replace_named_arg` for fact-shaped terms — ad-hoc reflection builtins retire (kept only for unsorted Term inspection). Work: (a) propose syntax — pick between unicode arrows, ASCII brackets, or keyword forms; document scoping rules (no cross-stage variable capture for v1); (b) grammar + converter; (c) typer pass: cross-stage elaboration assigning T to ↓t and Term to ↑e; (d) lowering: ↓t to alloc_from_value+as_entity, ↑v to TermPrinter or alloc_from_value depending on shape; (e) revise stdlib reflect declarations so the brackets are the primary surface; (f) port cmd_claim / cmd_deliver / cmd_verify to use ↓ / ↑ + WorkItem.copy (depends on WI-188 for the copy form). Acceptance: cmd_claim's body collapses to ~10 lines using ↓old_term / wi.copy(...) / ↑new_wi; the fresh_var / make_fn / replace_named_arg builtins get marked deprecated; full anthill-core suite green. Independent of WI-190 (quasi-quote patterns) — lands without that and works for non-pattern uses."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-188"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
